## Key Contextual Notes & Caveats

<div style="border-left:4px solid #F59E0B;background:#FFFBEB;padding:8px 12px;margin:8px 0;border-radius:4px;">
<strong>1/n accounting effect on IGAAP PAT:</strong> The statutory IGAAP PAT for 9MFY26 (<span style="color:#EF4444;font-weight:600">-₹214.4 Cr</span>) is negative largely because, w.e.f. Oct 1, 2024, IRDAI mandates deferral of long-term multi-year premiums via 1/n (50% UPR-plus-1/n) accounting, which suppresses earned premium recognition versus written premium — this contrasts sharply with the IFRS PAT of <span style="color:#22C55E;font-weight:600">+₹208.3 Cr</span>, which management explicitly says is "the right way to look at our financials" per CEO Krishnan Ramachandran on the Q3'FY26 earnings call <doc_source>step14web3</doc_source>.
</div>

- **GWP dual reporting:** Total GWP "without 1/n" for 9MFY26 was **₹6,309.4 Cr** (<span style="color:#22C55E;font-weight:600">▲ +25.9% YoY</span>) vs. the statutory 1/n-basis figure of **₹5,706.3 Cr** (<span style="color:#22C55E;font-weight:600">▲ +21.8% YoY</span>) used in the table above per task instructions.

<div style="border-left:4px solid #EF4444;background:#FEF2F2;padding:8px 12px;margin:8px 0;border-radius:4px;">
<strong>Solvency ratio decline:</strong> Fell to <span style="color:#EF4444;font-weight:600">2.49x</span> (Dec-25) from <span style="color:#22C55E;font-weight:600">3.03x</span> (Dec-24), driven by the 1/n accounting change suppressing IGAAP earnings. Management stated Q4 seasonality typically lifts IGAAP profit/solvency and does not view sub-2.0x as a near-term risk.
</div>

- **One-off items:** 9MFY26 figures include a ~₹20 Cr one-off IGAAP/IFRS profit hit from Code on Social Security, 2020 (gratuity/leave encashment) implementation effective Nov 21, 2025 — excluding this, the IFRS Combined Insurance Service Ratio (CISR) would be **102.6%** vs. the reported **102.9%**.

**Combined ratio divergence:** IGAAP Combined Ratio (1/n basis) deteriorated sharply to <span style="color:#EF4444;font-weight:600">▲ 111.9%</span> (9MFY26) from 105.0% (9MFY25), reflecting the earnings-drag mismatch from 1/n premium deferral rather than genuine underwriting deterioration. By contrast, the IFRS-basis Combined Insurance Service Ratio — a truer economic measure per management — instead **improved** to <span style="color:#22C55E;font-weight:600">▼ 102.9%</span> from 103.4%.

<div style="border-left:4px solid #1E3A8A;background:#EFF6FF;padding:8px 12px;margin:8px 0;border-radius:4px;">
<strong>Bottom line:</strong> Niva Bupa's 9MFY26 headline story bifurcates sharply by accounting basis. Statutory IGAAP metrics (PAT, Combined Ratio, Solvency) look weak purely due to the regulatory 1/n premium-deferral mandate, while the IFRS view — which management champions as economically representative — shows continued profitability and a stable-to-improving underwriting ratio. Analysts comparing this filing to peers should note which basis is being used, as the two paint materially different pictures for the same underlying business.
</div></ans>
